00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:03.260
And this is my code.

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:04.900
It's my favorite saying.

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:07.570
And my next favorite
saying is, "Applications

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:09.860
go wherever the data resides."

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:13.390
It's the data that creates
applications' stickiness

00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:14.890
to a platform.

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:17.170
You may start with
data monetization

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:22.270
or without monetization,
but just don't stop at one,

00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:26.650
but rather let one
drive the other, OK?

00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:29.230
Now, what's happening
in the data world--

00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:32.830
and we have been saying the
same story for a long time.

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:37.240
Data is just growing and
growing and growing, OK?

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:38.830
It's growing in volume.

00:01:38.830 --> 00:01:41.950
It's growing in its
velocity and the variety--

00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:44.230
the same old story, right?

00:01:44.230 --> 00:01:49.900
And now it's growing more with
all the digital transformation

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:54.250
that's happening, which is
propelled by the COVID, OK?

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:57.230
I was looking at the
fintech industry.

00:01:57.230 --> 00:02:01.600
It's one of the largest
vertical segments that we have.

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:06.610
It's handling 100 trillion
transactions a day

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:12.280
from around 20,000 financial
institutions worldwide.

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:15.760
And that's also unfortunately
driving the rise

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:18.820
in cybersecurity attacks.

00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:23.920
And there's a report from FBI
around how that has increased.

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:26.410
Now, that's a
number we don't want

00:02:26.410 --> 00:02:29.230
where it is right now
and directionally where

00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:31.060
the number is going.

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:35.440
We are also seeing that
customers are coming

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:38.140
to Cloud in different patterns.

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:43.940
Some are coming just as
is for reducing the costs.

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:47.560
So they're taking their existing
databases, moving it as is.

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:51.010
We call those homogeneous
database migrations.

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:53.560
And this could be
your existing Oracles

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:58.330
and SQL server, or even in the
open source world, your MySQL

00:02:58.330 --> 00:02:59.890
and PostgreSQL.

00:02:59.890 --> 00:03:04.030
So you bring it as is to
Cloud, run it in the Compute,

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:07.000
or you take it to
the Managed Services.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:11.590
That's definitely one
pattern that we are seeing.

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:14.680
As customers get
familiar with Cloud,

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:16.900
some move on to the next step.

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:18.940
And they say, I'm
going to break away

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:23.830
from these legacy databases and
use a cloud-native database.

00:03:23.830 --> 00:03:25.810
And that's another pattern.

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:30.760
But really, the pattern that has
picked up within the last year

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:34.000
is that customers
are saying, we really

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:35.950
want to try out a
new business model.

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:37.660
We know the power of Cloud.

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:41.830
We know the power of the
Google Cloud-native databases.

00:03:41.830 --> 00:03:46.960
And we want to transform
our customer experience.

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:51.800
We have solutions for all
these three customer journeys.

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:54.340
There's migration
solution that will help

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:56.500
you migrate your databases.

00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:59.920
There's modernization
solutions that

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:04.450
will help you do a
heterogeneous database

00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:10.510
modernization, for example,
from MySQL to Spanner or Oracle

00:04:10.510 --> 00:04:11.620
to Spanner.

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:15.040
And then really, what
today's focus in my talk

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:19.570
will be on transformation,
where the customers are

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:23.380
trying to redesign their
business models, OK?

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:27.542
So I lost something here.

00:04:27.542 --> 00:04:29.565
Let me turn off that.

00:04:29.565 --> 00:04:30.065
Sorry.

00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:41.240
So on the transformation side,
when we speak with customers,

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:44.470
they have told us,
we love to work

00:04:44.470 --> 00:04:47.290
with the Cloud-native databases.

00:04:47.290 --> 00:04:51.280
But we don't have
that critical mass

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:56.050
of developers who can develop
a workload, a new workload,

00:04:56.050 --> 00:04:58.990
a new transformative
workload, using

00:04:58.990 --> 00:05:00.940
those Cloud-native databases.

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:03.280
And yes, our
migration cycles are

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:05.860
long because it's not just
flipping the switch over

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:06.880
to database.

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:09.460
We got to look at the
application ecosystem.

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:10.480
We got to test it.

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:12.040
We got to certify it.

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:15.260
We got to make sure our
compliances are all there.

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:20.980
So this becomes kind of a
9 to 12-month cycle, right?

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:27.310
And then it's like, what can we
do to have something quicker?

00:05:27.310 --> 00:05:28.950
So they asked us for help.

00:05:28.950 --> 00:05:31.440
And yes, indeed, we gave help.

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:34.050
So this is the build
with Google's solution

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:35.640
from Google Cloud.

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:38.610
Basically in this
solution, we are giving you

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:40.200
the building blocks.

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:42.660
So think of it like
if you were building

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:44.910
a next-generation spaceship.

00:05:44.910 --> 00:05:48.420
You can build it with the
smallest brick, the smallest

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:51.930
block from LEGO or
anyone else, right?

00:05:51.930 --> 00:05:53.250
And you will get there.

00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:54.660
It will be a slow journey.

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:56.310
You will have some fallbacks.

00:05:56.310 --> 00:05:58.500
You will have some
risk to undertake.

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:00.640
But eventually,
you'll get there.

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:04.920
But what if you got
some prebuilt wings

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:08.340
of that spaceship or
engine or the nose.

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:10.980
And these prebuilt
hardened components,

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:12.300
you could use as is.

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:16.920
You could customize them
for your specifications.

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:21.330
That will help you build
a spaceship faster, right?

00:06:21.330 --> 00:06:23.700
It will accelerate
your journey there.

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:26.970
And also, because these
prebuilt components

00:06:26.970 --> 00:06:29.850
are from a vendor
like Google, it

00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:32.670
will mitigate your risk
because these are hardened.

00:06:32.670 --> 00:06:34.170
These are tested blocks.

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:36.240
Now, think of a
database application

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:38.880
instead of the
next-generation spaceship.

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:45.040
Every database application
has need to ingest data.

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:48.280
Then it has a need to make
sure the data is secure.

00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:50.430
It has a need to
process the data.

00:06:50.430 --> 00:06:53.220
It has a need to
analyze the data,

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:57.490
archive, delete, put
it on a dashboard.

00:06:57.490 --> 00:07:00.000
Those are like
common data services

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:04.830
that every application, every
database application needs.

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:10.650
And what happens today is that
as customers start building,

00:07:10.650 --> 00:07:12.540
they have to build all of that.

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:15.420
And this is what Build
with Google is doing.

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:20.340
It's handing over those
prebuilt blocks to the customers

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:26.650
so that they can accelerate
the journey to the cloud, OK?

00:07:26.650 --> 00:07:30.310
So in [INAUDIBLE]
first use case has been

00:07:30.310 --> 00:07:32.620
how to build a payment gateway.

00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:36.580
And we'll go over what
a payment gateway is,

00:07:36.580 --> 00:07:39.170
and we'll look at the
architecture for that.

00:07:39.170 --> 00:07:41.470
But basically,
this was a request

00:07:41.470 --> 00:07:43.330
from our retail customers.

00:07:43.330 --> 00:07:46.960
One of the common problems
of a retail customer

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:49.390
is the abandonment of the cart.

00:07:49.390 --> 00:07:52.660
And I've been guilty of
that as much as anyone else

00:07:52.660 --> 00:07:55.930
when I have to give my
credit card information

00:07:55.930 --> 00:07:57.850
and my address, 25 clicks.

00:07:57.850 --> 00:07:59.770
I'm like, oh, forget it.

00:07:59.770 --> 00:08:02.050
I'm not going to buy from here.

00:08:02.050 --> 00:08:05.830
70% of the customers abandon
their cart at checkout,

00:08:05.830 --> 00:08:09.430
and this is true for
the brick stores as well

00:08:09.430 --> 00:08:10.600
as online stores.

00:08:10.600 --> 00:08:13.540
And if you go and
talk to any retailer,

00:08:13.540 --> 00:08:15.670
they will give you
the exact dollar

00:08:15.670 --> 00:08:19.120
amount that it costs them, OK?

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:21.040
So that's a pure retail problem.

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:24.010
But then that
problem also extends

00:08:24.010 --> 00:08:28.240
into the financial sectors
where the traditional banks

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:31.360
are looking for their
next million customers.

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:35.890
And they're coming to Google,
and we have only just a few,

00:08:35.890 --> 00:08:40.880
to say, apps with billions
of customers using them.

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:45.220
And they're looking for
some new applications that

00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:50.080
will get all the attention of
those millennial customers,

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:55.240
make them loyal customers
while being compliant.

00:08:55.240 --> 00:08:58.780
Compliance is a very,
very important thing

00:08:58.780 --> 00:09:01.110
in financial industry.

00:09:01.110 --> 00:09:05.040
So our payment gateway use
case has a proven reference

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:10.470
architecture that's been
built on top of the learnings

00:09:10.470 --> 00:09:13.210
from our own Google Payments.

00:09:13.210 --> 00:09:16.260
If you have ever been
a customer of Google,

00:09:16.260 --> 00:09:20.430
whether Google Play, YouTube,
or a Cloud subscription,

00:09:20.430 --> 00:09:24.570
any financial transaction that
you would have done with Google

00:09:24.570 --> 00:09:27.090
runs on that Google
Payments platform.

00:09:27.090 --> 00:09:30.270
So we have gone and
learned from that

00:09:30.270 --> 00:09:35.700
and incorporated those learnings
in that reference architecture

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:40.080
and make sure that we shared
it with the customer using

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:44.280
the Google Cloud
technologies like Spanner.

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:47.100
Now, this Google
Payment handles hundreds

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:49.140
of millions of transactions.

00:09:49.140 --> 00:09:53.430
It runs in 200-plus
countries around the world,

00:09:53.430 --> 00:09:57.450
handling multiple
currencies, doing compliance

00:09:57.450 --> 00:10:01.890
across all these countries, so
financial reporting, compliance

00:10:01.890 --> 00:10:07.710
reporting, treasury reporting,
currency hedging, all of that.

00:10:07.710 --> 00:10:10.950
Now, we have taken a
small component of it,

00:10:10.950 --> 00:10:15.530
and I'll go over that in a
short while with all of you.

00:10:15.530 --> 00:10:19.280
The next use case is
basically a data fabric.

00:10:19.280 --> 00:10:22.160
By the way, the payment
gateway reference architecture

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:25.250
is available today,
the version 1 of it.

00:10:25.250 --> 00:10:28.880
For version 2, we
are doing more work,

00:10:28.880 --> 00:10:31.760
pulling together the
data abstraction layer,

00:10:31.760 --> 00:10:35.360
also double [INAUDIBLE] into
the risk management system,

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:37.280
and also put in some blueprints.

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:39.990
So we'll go over those shortly.

00:10:39.990 --> 00:10:44.600
But let me introduce our second
use case before we move on,

00:10:44.600 --> 00:10:48.320
and that's building
a data fabric, right?

00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:53.000
So a lot of banks,
as they are trying

00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:56.720
to implement the open
banking standards,

00:10:56.720 --> 00:10:59.570
they need to move fast, right?

00:10:59.570 --> 00:11:03.080
Customers in retail and
manufacturing need to have

00:11:03.080 --> 00:11:07.140
a real-time view of their
entire supply chain,

00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:10.160
not just one part of it,
but the whole thing--

00:11:10.160 --> 00:11:14.000
supply chain, warehouse,
their partners,

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:18.770
their suppliers, down to the
root, right-- and the order

00:11:18.770 --> 00:11:22.600
view and what's selling
their view, right?

00:11:22.600 --> 00:11:30.110
If you walk into a store and
see an out-of-stock item, that's

00:11:30.110 --> 00:11:33.860
a lot of lost sales for
that retailer, right?

00:11:33.860 --> 00:11:36.590
And every retailer will
tell you exactly the dollar

00:11:36.590 --> 00:11:37.650
amount of it.

00:11:37.650 --> 00:11:41.480
But not just in retail,
telecom service providers,

00:11:41.480 --> 00:11:46.670
as you subscribe to a plan,
the call detail record

00:11:46.670 --> 00:11:50.030
tells a lot about
your calling patterns

00:11:50.030 --> 00:11:55.400
and can give the telecom service
provider some real insights

00:11:55.400 --> 00:12:00.590
into fraud and prevent those
fraud across the globe, OK?

00:12:00.590 --> 00:12:05.210
So having that CDR, having
the health care provider have

00:12:05.210 --> 00:12:10.620
a 360-degree view of the
patients in real time--

00:12:10.620 --> 00:12:14.530
these are some of the needs
coming from our customers.

00:12:14.530 --> 00:12:16.660
And what we have
with the data fabric

00:12:16.660 --> 00:12:19.210
is a proven reference
architecture

00:12:19.210 --> 00:12:22.600
with building blocks for
the common data services

00:12:22.600 --> 00:12:24.370
so that we reference
architecture

00:12:24.370 --> 00:12:28.810
that record snippets
available in a Google Cloud

00:12:28.810 --> 00:12:31.950
repo, documentation,
installation

00:12:31.950 --> 00:12:35.200
scripts, and in
some cases a sandbox

00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:38.080
where you can come
and play with this.

00:12:38.080 --> 00:12:41.410
These common data services
will help you do data ingestion

00:12:41.410 --> 00:12:44.680
in real time, catalog
management, metadata

00:12:44.680 --> 00:12:47.890
management, data
discovery, data lineage

00:12:47.890 --> 00:12:50.260
integration with
security services,

00:12:50.260 --> 00:12:53.740
integration with analytics
and dashboard and reporting

00:12:53.740 --> 00:12:58.360
services, using the Google
Cloud-native databases.

00:12:58.360 --> 00:13:00.580
And then once you have
that infrastructure,

00:13:00.580 --> 00:13:07.600
we expect the customers to just
focus on their vertical use

00:13:07.600 --> 00:13:11.790
case, their business logic when
they want to build that fraud

00:13:11.790 --> 00:13:12.550
application.

00:13:12.550 --> 00:13:16.180
They don't have to worry about
these common data services.

00:13:16.180 --> 00:13:18.530
They get that from
the data fabric.

00:13:18.530 --> 00:13:20.830
They have to worry
about, what's my logic?

00:13:20.830 --> 00:13:22.090
What are my rules?

00:13:22.090 --> 00:13:28.900
What are my triggers to do
fraud alerts, to do an order,

00:13:28.900 --> 00:13:35.290
to do a pricing promotion, any
of those vertical use cases?

00:13:35.290 --> 00:13:39.520
For the gaming segment, we plan
to publish some code templates.

00:13:39.520 --> 00:13:42.670
We're using Spanner
and Firestore

00:13:42.670 --> 00:13:47.410
for the retail apart from
real-time recommendations

00:13:47.410 --> 00:13:50.230
and personalizations,
order management,

00:13:50.230 --> 00:13:54.170
inventory management, a
view of the supply chain.

00:13:54.170 --> 00:13:58.630
So today, we are going
out with financial use

00:13:58.630 --> 00:14:00.950
cases and retail use cases.

00:14:00.950 --> 00:14:04.360
But we are also keeping our
eye on health care and life

00:14:04.360 --> 00:14:09.490
sciences for a patient 360, CDR
store for telecommunications,

00:14:09.490 --> 00:14:12.700
bill of materials
for manufacturing.

00:14:12.700 --> 00:14:14.080
Just watch this space.

00:14:14.080 --> 00:14:16.930
There's a lot coming in.

00:14:16.930 --> 00:14:20.140
Let me move on to
the payments gateway.

00:14:20.140 --> 00:14:23.440
For those of you who
have used payments,

00:14:23.440 --> 00:14:27.400
any credit card, debit
card-- it gets acquired.

00:14:27.400 --> 00:14:30.860
You may be doing a
point-of-sale terminal.

00:14:30.860 --> 00:14:34.480
It will get acquired, and
then processing happens.

00:14:34.480 --> 00:14:37.870
And then it gets handed
over to a payments

00:14:37.870 --> 00:14:41.980
processor, which will then work
with the payer bank and payee

00:14:41.980 --> 00:14:44.690
bank to settle that transaction.

00:14:44.690 --> 00:14:47.470
So there are a lot of
components behind this

00:14:47.470 --> 00:14:50.170
which make this happen.

00:14:50.170 --> 00:14:55.720
And things like when you
acquired the transaction,

00:14:55.720 --> 00:14:59.350
the very first thing
we do is to encrypt it

00:14:59.350 --> 00:15:01.990
because it has your PII, right?

00:15:01.990 --> 00:15:06.040
You don't want to show that
credit card in real time.

00:15:06.040 --> 00:15:10.060
And then you would have
some orchestration services.

00:15:10.060 --> 00:15:13.750
You will be provisioning the
users, verifying the users.

00:15:13.750 --> 00:15:16.780
The payment management
system will be going deeper

00:15:16.780 --> 00:15:19.390
into the payment transaction.

00:15:19.390 --> 00:15:23.350
It will be doing some risk
assessment in real time,

00:15:23.350 --> 00:15:25.420
whether to approve
the transaction

00:15:25.420 --> 00:15:29.860
or deny it, or maybe cap
it based on your profile.

00:15:29.860 --> 00:15:32.110
All of this is
happening and getting

00:15:32.110 --> 00:15:36.940
stored in a secure data
storage which is PCI compliant

00:15:36.940 --> 00:15:41.590
before it gets handed over
to the payment processor, OK?

00:15:41.590 --> 00:15:45.430
So between these two vertical
lines, everything in between

00:15:45.430 --> 00:15:48.190
is the scope of a
payments gateway

00:15:48.190 --> 00:15:50.710
for which we have put
together the reference

00:15:50.710 --> 00:15:53.860
architecture for
you to use it with

00:15:53.860 --> 00:15:56.020
deep technical documentation.

00:15:56.020 --> 00:15:59.320
In this reference
architecture, we

00:15:59.320 --> 00:16:03.010
are using GKE for all
the application services,

00:16:03.010 --> 00:16:06.940
and we are using Cloud Spanner
for all your database needs

00:16:06.940 --> 00:16:10.420
because as you will
see, the tenets that we

00:16:10.420 --> 00:16:13.520
had for this design
around security,

00:16:13.520 --> 00:16:16.450
around availability,
around consistency,

00:16:16.450 --> 00:16:18.580
the only product
in Cloud that could

00:16:18.580 --> 00:16:20.770
meet it was Cloud Spanner.

00:16:20.770 --> 00:16:25.990
So talking about design
tenets, what we had was--

00:16:25.990 --> 00:16:34.750
first and foremost, it was
the PCI compliance, right?

00:16:34.750 --> 00:16:39.250
And basically, it was to
maintain the secure network,

00:16:39.250 --> 00:16:43.330
maintain the cardholder
data, monitor it--

00:16:43.330 --> 00:16:47.260
who's accessing what,
log it, and audit it.

00:16:47.260 --> 00:16:51.070
But apart from security
itself, it also

00:16:51.070 --> 00:16:56.230
needs to be highly available
because you need access

00:16:56.230 --> 00:16:58.480
to your money at all times.

00:16:58.480 --> 00:17:03.190
And your transaction needs to
have total integrity, right?

00:17:03.190 --> 00:17:06.310
If you are doing a
transaction for $40,

00:17:06.310 --> 00:17:12.069
it better not be $40.0001, OK?

00:17:12.069 --> 00:17:14.560
And then have performance,
and not only performance,

00:17:14.560 --> 00:17:18.819
but performance at scale, the
throughput and the latency,

00:17:18.819 --> 00:17:21.760
and then the manageability
aspects, the backup,

00:17:21.760 --> 00:17:25.270
and the restore verification
and the disaster recovery.

00:17:25.270 --> 00:17:29.980
And it should be able to scale
up and down as your scale

00:17:29.980 --> 00:17:33.670
increases or you go through
a quiet period, right?

00:17:33.670 --> 00:17:34.700
All of that.

00:17:34.700 --> 00:17:38.410
So Cloud Spanner was
the only database

00:17:38.410 --> 00:17:42.220
that we could find which met
all of those design tenets.

00:17:42.220 --> 00:17:44.230
It gave us security
and compliance

00:17:44.230 --> 00:17:50.290
out of the box, DLP, ANSI
SQL, very, very strong

00:17:50.290 --> 00:17:54.190
external consistency, scale
of petabytes of database

00:17:54.190 --> 00:18:00.760
with performance, distributed,
strong consistency, very simple

00:18:00.760 --> 00:18:04.540
administration,
online schema changes,

00:18:04.540 --> 00:18:08.290
five-nines availability with the
architecture that I'm sure you

00:18:08.290 --> 00:18:10.750
have heard about, RPO = 0.

00:18:10.750 --> 00:18:14.020
In a financial, in any of
these transactional industry,

00:18:14.020 --> 00:18:18.250
you cannot, cannot
lose any data, OK?

00:18:18.250 --> 00:18:23.560
And failure recovery,
and on a global scale,

00:18:23.560 --> 00:18:25.750
but with multiregions.

00:18:25.750 --> 00:18:27.940
On the security and
compliance side,

00:18:27.940 --> 00:18:30.370
the shared responsibility
model, very well

00:18:30.370 --> 00:18:32.800
documented on our website.

00:18:32.800 --> 00:18:39.040
For PCI DSS, how do
you isolate the data?

00:18:39.040 --> 00:18:42.550
How do you encrypt at
rest and in transit?

00:18:42.550 --> 00:18:46.690
How do you manage your
keys with KMS and HSM?

00:18:46.690 --> 00:18:48.580
How do you do logging?

00:18:48.580 --> 00:18:52.750
Who is doing what and
audit and monitor and do

00:18:52.750 --> 00:19:00.130
the PCI-DSS-compliant templates?

00:19:00.130 --> 00:19:05.890
Spanner integrates with IAM
for fine-grained access.

00:19:05.890 --> 00:19:08.590
It integrates with
VPC-SC to achieve

00:19:08.590 --> 00:19:12.100
isolation of cardholder data.

00:19:12.100 --> 00:19:13.780
It encrypts the data.

00:19:13.780 --> 00:19:16.510
You can manage your
keys with the CMEK

00:19:16.510 --> 00:19:18.580
and also audit logging
and monitoring.

00:19:18.580 --> 00:19:21.610
I'm going a little bit faster,
keeping an eye on the time.

00:19:21.610 --> 00:19:25.180
There are these PCI DSS
blueprints available

00:19:25.180 --> 00:19:30.130
today for PCI on GKE
and Anthos, and Spanner

00:19:30.130 --> 00:19:31.490
is coming very soon.

00:19:31.490 --> 00:19:33.460
You will see it announced.

00:19:33.460 --> 00:19:35.650
On the scale and
availability, I want

00:19:35.650 --> 00:19:41.440
to point out the autoscaling
that Spanner has.

00:19:41.440 --> 00:19:44.640
There are multiple ways to
create the regional cluster,

00:19:44.640 --> 00:19:47.020
the standard or the autopilot.

00:19:47.020 --> 00:19:52.390
And then we handle both
zonal failures as well as

00:19:52.390 --> 00:19:53.260
regional failures.

00:19:53.260 --> 00:19:56.860
And knock on wood-- we have
not had a regional failure.

00:19:56.860 --> 00:19:59.320
Same thing with
Spanner with Autoscaler

00:19:59.320 --> 00:20:04.240
and multiregion configuration
and replication and DR--

00:20:04.240 --> 00:20:08.830
all that is coming out
of the box for your use.

00:20:08.830 --> 00:20:11.890
The data fabric itself,
like the lifecycle

00:20:11.890 --> 00:20:14.950
of data from ingestion
to processing

00:20:14.950 --> 00:20:18.820
to storing to analyzing and
then getting value out of it,

00:20:18.820 --> 00:20:22.240
across the board with
security and governance

00:20:22.240 --> 00:20:26.350
underlying all those
services is what we have.

00:20:26.350 --> 00:20:28.750
Here is an
architecture that could

00:20:28.750 --> 00:20:33.040
be data coming from a number
of sources in batch mode,

00:20:33.040 --> 00:20:37.120
in streaming, real time,
structured, unstructured

00:20:37.120 --> 00:20:39.130
data, semi-structured data.

00:20:39.130 --> 00:20:43.270
We collect all of that
in a giant real-time,

00:20:43.270 --> 00:20:47.440
time-series-ordered,
synchronized, distributed event

00:20:47.440 --> 00:20:50.590
store built on top of Spanner.

00:20:50.590 --> 00:20:52.750
If you don't have
the requirements

00:20:52.750 --> 00:20:54.910
that Spanner needs,
like you don't need

00:20:54.910 --> 00:20:57.610
that strong consistency,
but you need

00:20:57.610 --> 00:21:00.830
very low latency and a
very high throughput,

00:21:00.830 --> 00:21:04.930
this architecture will
also work on Bigtable.

00:21:04.930 --> 00:21:07.150
You then give it to
the data services

00:21:07.150 --> 00:21:10.390
that you could be running in
GKE to do your processing.

00:21:10.390 --> 00:21:14.890
You can give it to BigQuery
to do the pattern analytics

00:21:14.890 --> 00:21:17.950
and to run those BQML models.

00:21:17.950 --> 00:21:21.040
Or archive it in
Google Cloud Storage

00:21:21.040 --> 00:21:25.090
because for a lot
of industries, there

00:21:25.090 --> 00:21:28.420
are requirements to keep
the data for 7 years, 10

00:21:28.420 --> 00:21:31.010
years, or life long,
like in health care.

00:21:31.010 --> 00:21:34.800
And you can explore the data
on the dashboards using Looker.

00:21:34.800 --> 00:21:37.150
So this entire
range of services--

00:21:37.150 --> 00:21:39.460
here is a reference
architecture for data fabric

00:21:39.460 --> 00:21:41.050
that we will be building.

00:21:41.050 --> 00:21:43.780
OK now, here are some
of the requirements

00:21:43.780 --> 00:21:45.680
across the industries.

00:21:45.680 --> 00:21:48.970
So for retail, you definitely
need the performance

00:21:48.970 --> 00:21:52.720
at scale, especially on a
Black Friday, Cyber Monday.

00:21:52.720 --> 00:21:57.590
You also need a single source of
truth across all the channels.

00:21:57.590 --> 00:22:00.910
Whether you are ordering it
with your app or on the web

00:22:00.910 --> 00:22:05.770
or in the store, if it tells
you, Ajaya, you have 25 doors.

00:22:05.770 --> 00:22:09.310
I don't need 25 doors,
25 hinges, right?

00:22:09.310 --> 00:22:12.070
Then I should be
able to get those 25

00:22:12.070 --> 00:22:14.110
hinges at a consistent price.

00:22:14.110 --> 00:22:16.480
It should not be that different
channels are giving me

00:22:16.480 --> 00:22:18.310
different prices, right?

00:22:18.310 --> 00:22:20.920
I should be able to
shop any time I need.

00:22:20.920 --> 00:22:25.060
I don't want to see
"come back later," right?

00:22:25.060 --> 00:22:28.630
Every retailer will tell you
what's the cost of downtime.

00:22:28.630 --> 00:22:32.530
And most of them have
staff trained in ANSI SQL,

00:22:32.530 --> 00:22:35.230
so having that with
Spanner definitely helps.

00:22:35.230 --> 00:22:38.290
Similar requirements
with gaming--

00:22:38.290 --> 00:22:40.960
hundreds of very
popular game needs

00:22:40.960 --> 00:22:44.200
to manage hundreds of
VMs, thousands of VMs,

00:22:44.200 --> 00:22:46.570
and you don't want to
be doing that manually.

00:22:46.570 --> 00:22:48.970
And the game, especially
with multiplayer

00:22:48.970 --> 00:22:52.120
games located in different
countries-- the games

00:22:52.120 --> 00:22:54.910
have to be available
at all times, at scale,

00:22:54.910 --> 00:22:57.460
and not cost you a [INAUDIBLE]

00:22:57.460 --> 00:23:02.590
Fintech, again, all of those,
as well as availability, as well

00:23:02.590 --> 00:23:07.390
as compliance, as well as strong
consistency in a distributed

00:23:07.390 --> 00:23:12.460
environment across multiple
geographic regions--

00:23:12.460 --> 00:23:13.220
all of that.

00:23:13.220 --> 00:23:15.850
So here is another
architecture very

00:23:15.850 --> 00:23:18.220
similar to the data
fabric architecture.

00:23:18.220 --> 00:23:21.760
You could be doing your banking
from any of these sources.

00:23:21.760 --> 00:23:23.380
You write your APIs.

00:23:23.380 --> 00:23:27.670
You have your transactional
apps running on top of Spanner.

00:23:27.670 --> 00:23:30.460
The transaction goes out
to the payment gateway,

00:23:30.460 --> 00:23:33.910
which hinges a doorway
to the payment processor

00:23:33.910 --> 00:23:37.750
or the core banking, a
legacy core banking system.

00:23:37.750 --> 00:23:41.290
There is security and
governance underneath all this.

00:23:41.290 --> 00:23:43.750
And then you can
run your analytics

00:23:43.750 --> 00:23:45.910
and collect the data
in a data warehouse.

00:23:45.910 --> 00:23:49.930
This architecture is actually
working at multiple customers.

00:23:49.930 --> 00:23:52.570
Our typical winning
profile has been--

00:23:52.570 --> 00:23:54.710
and this is what
I mean to say is,

00:23:54.710 --> 00:23:59.200
why did these customers in
these segments chose Spanner?

00:23:59.200 --> 00:24:01.660
In the gaming,
strong consistency.

00:24:01.660 --> 00:24:06.100
Leaderboard requires very
strong consistency, right?

00:24:06.100 --> 00:24:08.110
If I'm the leader, I'm
the top of the game,

00:24:08.110 --> 00:24:10.540
I want to be there, right?

00:24:10.540 --> 00:24:13.630
A very popular game has
to scale immediately.

00:24:13.630 --> 00:24:15.340
And when there is
no usage, it has

00:24:15.340 --> 00:24:17.450
to scale down automatically.

00:24:17.450 --> 00:24:18.730
It got to be available.

00:24:18.730 --> 00:24:20.710
Someone is playing in Taiwan.

00:24:20.710 --> 00:24:23.410
Someone is playing in Argentina.

00:24:23.410 --> 00:24:25.900
It got to be available
at all times.

00:24:25.900 --> 00:24:29.050
You have no tolerance
for downtime,

00:24:29.050 --> 00:24:31.480
got to be able to
change your schema

00:24:31.480 --> 00:24:35.215
and manage your
shards automatically.

00:24:35.215 --> 00:24:40.300
Finserv, all of that, plus very
strong consistent transactions,

00:24:40.300 --> 00:24:43.180
no tolerance for RPO, right?

00:24:43.180 --> 00:24:44.590
No tolerance for that.

00:24:44.590 --> 00:24:48.370
Autoscale some of the
performance scales where 23

00:24:48.370 --> 00:24:52.510
million customers doing 25
million transactions a day

00:24:52.510 --> 00:24:56.770
at 10-millisecond-or-less
latency.

00:24:56.770 --> 00:25:00.470
In the retail, same thing--
multiregion, consistency,

00:25:00.470 --> 00:25:01.700
availability.

00:25:01.700 --> 00:25:06.160
I'm just a broken record
